        "--title",
        "comparison launch",
        "lib/parse.js dispatches its parser states through a `parseStates` object whose methods (start, beforePropertyName, afterPropertyName, beforePropertyValue, beforeArrayValue, afterPropertyValue, afterArrayValue, end) the parse loop calls as parseStates[parseState](). The lexer states in the same file were already converted from such a table to `function processLexState (state)` holding a single `switch (state)`. Convert the parser states the same way, for performance: replace the object with `function processParseState (state) {` containing one `switch (state)` with a block-scoped case per state (`case 'start': {` and so on) at the same four-space indentation the lexer's cases use, each case returning where the method returned, and call it as `processParseState(parseState)` from the loop. Structural requirements, checked by regular expression on lib/parse.js after the change: no line starts with `const parseStates`; no uncommented `parseStates[` remains; `function processParseState (state) {` occurs exactly once at the start of a line and `processParseState(parseState)` exactly once as a statement of its own; and each of the eight state names occurs exactly twice as a line `    case '<name>': {` (once in the lexer's switch, once in the new one). The state bodies and every parse result and error are unchanged. npm test (which builds, runs the node test suite and enforces 100% line, branch and function coverage of lib, so no unreachable default branch may be added) and npm run lint must pass. Change only lib/parse.js (a CHANGELOG.md entry is welcome); do not edit tests.\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npm test\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/."
      ],
